[Music]  many Americans are struggling with the  consequences of stay-at-home orders  during this pandemic calls to the  government's mental health hotline were  up nearly 900 percent in the month of  March from the same month last year and  we're also seeing a rise in both  antidepressant and anti-anxiety  prescriptions CBS News mental health  contributor that's dr. su pharma joins  us to discuss su I can't believe that  I'm seeing you today of all days just  yesterday a friend sent me a number of a  psychiatrist and said Gayle you need to  make a call and I was a little taken  aback by that because I don't think what  I'm experiencing is any more than  anybody else so how do you know when  it's time to seek help asking for a  friend  Calvin yes Gayle what a great question  and you know the reality is that we are  all experiencing a wave of emotions that  might change from moment to moment and I  say to people that you might feel sad  one moment and might feel thankful  another moment and the reality is is  that grief and gratitude can coexist it  doesn't mean that there's something  wrong with you but how do we know when  it's a problem is when it starts to  interfere with your daily life so is a  low mood unshakable do your daily tasks  feel insurmountable and are the things  that were once fun to you no longer  pleasurable and to me that means that  this is an issue and also I ask people  about their sleep why because I think  that sleep is a window into your mental  health I think that a lot of people may  not experiencing anxiety or depression  during the day they may say I'm busy I'm  distracted I've got people to take care  of I've got things to do but how are you  sleeping not well I haven't slept in two  months doc that's what I'm hearing so I  think that we had problems sleeping even  before the average person might have  about thirty four percent of the time  have some sort of acute insomnia about  two-thirds or three-fourths of the time  it dissipates by itself but really we  want to see how people are sleeping  because to me that might be a better  hallmark than just asking them how  they're feeling well at what point does  staying at home take a toll on your  mental health it doesn't seem to me that  it's a one-size-fits-all on this  question no it really varies from person  to person and there was an interesting  Lancet review that came out at the end  of February that looked at  twenty four studies from ten countries  who had experienced different outbreaks  deadly outbreaks and one study from  Canada found that even ten days was  enough to increase the risk for having  post-traumatic stress disorder anger and  avoidance but really it's going to  depend on a variety of factors do you  have a history of mental illness to  begin with and then do you feel as if  you didn't get adequate information  about the pandemic if you were dealing  with one did you not have adequate  supplies were there financial losses so  all of this compounds away and will make  the decision as to how many days but you  know it really does vary from person to  person based on pre-existing conditions  including lack of social support well  Toni had a great tease when he was  talking about your appearance coming up  he said that you would have four steps  to help us with our mental health what  are they can you give us a boom  boom-boom-boom  yes so the four M's of mental health as  I call it is movement meaningful  engagement mastery and mindfulness and  these are just quick things that you can  do ten or fifteen minutes I'm a big  believer and if you can get outside  brisk walk wearing a mask social  distancing get adequate sunlight in the  daytime and this shuts off the melatonin  it tells that your brain that it's ready  for the day and believe it or not it  actually helps you sleep at night when  it comes to mindfulness spend five or  ten minutes doing deep breathing there  are tons of apps out there calm  headspace do whatever feels good for you  progressive muscle relaxation is one of  them when it comes to meaningful  engagement you have to connect with  people on any level on a deeper level on  a superficial level these days I can't  get to tell you how excited I get to go  to the grocery store once a week so that  I can see other people outside of my  family and those those little micro  conversations are really key but also  call text FaceTime do whatever you can  to have those deeper more vulnerable  conversations and then mastery do  something that feels good you don't have  to be a master to be experiencing  mastery and that's just something  creative a state of flow and the  difference between anxiety and  depression is anxiety is rumination or  worry about the future  a long  irritability trouble sleeping trouble  concentrating having physiological  reactions heart racing increased blood  pressure and it's very much focused  about the future what if depression on  the other hand often is about the past  and rumination and regret and feelings  of sadness worthlessness hopelessness  along with sleep problems and difficulty  concentrating and appetite a lot of  times anxiety and depression go  hand-in-hand  and obviously the medications what's  interesting is that antidepressants also  work for anxiety so you know it's very  important to talk to your doctor to  figure out which one you have and which  medication might be better because a lot  of the antidepressants are actually FDA  approved for anxiety medications alright  dr. sue Varma very good to see you  giving us a lot to think about really  appreciate that if you or someone you  know thank you if someone if you or  someone you know is seeking mental  health resources you can call the  national hotline there is a number for  you the National Alliance on Mental  Illness the helpline is one eight  hundred nine five Oh Nami one eight  hundred nine five Oh Nami or one eight  hundred nine five Oh 62 64 I think I  like it better that one that way or in a  crisis you can text them at 741 741

